/**
 * Phase 2D — Extension Visa research for the same 28 Tier-A countries
 * already researched for Tourist/Business/Transit/Family/Sports/Medical/
 * Crew. Content status is 'researched' (never 'verified'), for the same
 * WebFetch-blanket-blocked reason documented in every prior batch: facts
 * were gathered via WebSearch's cited synthesis, never independently
 * fetched against a primary source in this environment.
 *
 * CRITICAL GOVERNANCE PRINCIPLE: "Extension Visa" is a database content
 * category, NOT proof that a legal visa-extension product exists for any
 * given country. Every record in this batch distinguishes, in its own
 * content, which of several genuinely different legal mechanisms actually
 * applies: Extension Visa (a literal separate visa product) is DIFFERENT
 * from Extension of Stay (the same entry's authorized period extended
 * in-country), DIFFERENT from a Residence Permit (a different legal
 * instrument entirely), and DIFFERENT from simply requiring a brand-new
 * visa application because no extension exists at all. No country here
 * was forced into a "dedicated Extension Visa" outcome that is not
 * genuinely supported by research.
 *
 * Outcomes found, per country, span multiple distinct mechanisms:
 *   - Clean, well-documented EXTENSION OF STAY (same visa/entry's period
 *     extended in-country, not a new visa): Qatar (one-time 30-day
 *     extension via MOI/Metrash2), Oman (one-time 30-day extension via
 *     the ROP portal; 10-day eVisas are non-extendable), Thailand (TM.7
 *     form, 30 extra days, same-day, THB 1,900), Indonesia (Visa on
 *     Arrival extendable once for 30 more days via evisa.imigrasi.go.id,
 *     with a May-2025 in-person-photo requirement now layered on top of
 *     the online process), Kenya (eTA extendable to a 180-day/calendar-
 *     year cap via the eFNS portal), Maldives (up to a 90-day cap via
 *     IMUGA, with a separate, NOT-to-be-assumed-active 48-hour emergency
 *     measure disclosed distinctly), Mauritius (free, same-day, up to a
 *     180-day/calendar-year cap, in person only in Port Louis), Nepal
 *     (up to a 150-day/year cumulative cap, online form + mandatory
 *     in-person appearance, USD 45 + USD 3/day), Sri Lanka (staged
 *     60/90/90-day extensions up to a 270-day cumulative cap, in person
 *     only in Colombo, no online option).
 *   - EXTENSION OF STAY available only for SPECIFIC CATEGORIES, not the
 *     ordinary tourist entry (Case C): UAE (extension-of-stay via ICP/
 *     GDRFA, not a new visa, up to 120 days total), Singapore (STVP
 *     extension via ICA e-Service only, online-only, holistically
 *     assessed, up to 89 days from entry), Ireland (true extensions
 *     limited to narrow emergency cases, though a traveller granted
 *     fewer than 90 days at entry may separately request a top-up to the
 *     standard 90-day ceiling — a distinct, non-emergency sub-case kept
 *     separate from the emergency exception per Case-distinguishing
 *     governance).
 *   - EXTENSION POSSIBLE ONLY UNDER EXCEPTIONAL CIRCUMSTANCES (Case D),
 *     not a routine process: Germany, France, Italy, Spain, Netherlands,
 *     Switzerland (the Schengen six — Article 33 Visa Code grounds: force
 *     majeure, humanitarian reasons, or serious personal reasons; Italy
 *     via the Questura ONLY, not embassy/consulate; fee figures for
 *     Switzerland conflict across sources (CHF 60-150 vs EUR 30) and this
 *     conflict is disclosed rather than resolved), Japan (accident/
 *     illness/humanitarian grounds only via the regional Immigration
 *     Services Agency; a special 6-month visa-exempt allowance exists for
 *     six specific nationalities and explicitly EXCLUDES India), South
 *     Korea (a single extension within the original 90-day scope, for
 *     specific reasons only — unforeseen accident/illness preventing
 *     departure, or qualifying relative-visit/language-training purposes
 *     — general "more time" requests are typically declined), Malaysia
 *     (officially reserved for extreme circumstances only, though
 *     informal reports describe a more lenient practical pattern that
 *     should not be relied upon — this official/informal gap is disclosed
 *     rather than resolved).
 *   - MUST LEAVE AND OBTAIN A NEW VISA (Case B), no in-country extension
 *     at all for the entry category actually used: Saudi Arabia (tourist
 *     eVisa cannot be extended at all — must leave and reapply; a
 *     SEPARATE Visit Visa category, not the tourist eVisa, is extendable
 *     via Absher/Tawasul, and a temporary 30-day grace-period initiative
 *     found in research is explicitly flagged as NOT a standing policy),
 *     Turkey (tourist e-Visa/sticker visa explicitly cannot be extended
 *     per Turkey's own e-Visa portal FAQ — the only route to stay longer
 *     is a separate, different-mechanism short-term RESIDENCE PERMIT via
 *     the DGMM, not a visa extension), Vietnam (the e-Visa itself cannot
 *     be extended under any circumstance and requires an entirely new
 *     visa via an inviting/guaranteeing organization — but, kept
 *     deliberately separate and NOT conflated, a different, non-e-Visa
 *     standard travel visa CAN be extended in person for 15-30 days via
 *     Form NA5).
 *   - CONDITIONAL / DISCRETIONARY, not guaranteed, with a genuine fee
 *     conflict disclosed: Bahrain (14-day NPRA extension, discretionary,
 *     fee figures conflict across sources — BD 30/40/50 — disclosed
 *     rather than resolved).
 *   - MATERIAL SOURCE CONFLICT on whether extension is even possible at
 *     all, disclosed explicitly rather than guessed: Egypt (some sources
 *     describe a 30-90 day Mogamma-building extension process for the
 *     eVisa; another source states the eVisa cannot be extended at all
 *     and travellers must leave before expiry; a separate tourist
 *     residence permit route exists via the Abasya migration office as a
 *     genuinely different legal mechanism for a longer stay).
 *   - CURRENT-OPERATIONAL-STATUS CAVEAT disclosed on top of the standing
 *     legal mechanism: South Africa (Section 11(1) visitor-visa extension
 *     is the standing legal mechanism, up to 90 further days via VFS
 *     Global, but one source explicitly reports the Department of Home
 *     Affairs is not currently accepting extensions, meaning a new visa
 *     may be required instead right now — this time-sensitive finding is
 *     surfaced prominently, not buried under the standing legal position).
 *   - STANDARD EXTENSION-OF-STAY with a KNOWN, SEPARATELY-DISCLOSED
 *     TEMPORARY EMERGENCY MEASURE not to be assumed currently active:
 *     Kuwait (standard 90+90-day extension via the General Directorate of
 *     Residency; a separate automatic-extension measure tied to
 *     "exceptional regional circumstances" found in research is
 *     explicitly NOT assumed to be currently active).
 *
 * Document tiers, fee/processing hedging, and the biometric/interview
 * four-state governance model all follow the same conventions established
